rs he went in  when he was 15 years old  and  he was a scout until  he became 18  when he was placed in the 51st alabama  partisan rangers  he  i don't think was at fort pila  but he was with  general forest when  forest chased  straight across alabama and tennessee  and to georgia  and being the shrewd man that he was  he  made a ploy  that caused straight to surrender some  twelve thousand men to his seven  thousand right the only thing i say is  this  remember that a lot of us  who had people who fought in that war my  grandmother my grandfather on my  mother's side was with the 18th alabama  infantry  and  it was a fact that  a lot of  people who had slaves  fought it's also a fact that neither one  of my families had slaves  and they fought because  the northern troops invaded the south  and they were protecting their families  and their homes at least that's what  they thought  so i ask you to  be kind to forests  in your evaluations when you seeked  out the fact that many of them hated him  he  faced  general braxton bragg  after the battle in chickamauga georgia  and  told him that  he would never serve under him again  and that if he ever spoke to him again  he would do so at the peril of his life  right  simply because he would not  let them go on  and  capture the remnants of the federal  troops at that time  he was hated so bad by  general sherman  that he said  that we could never have peace even  after the southerners had given up until  he was able to execute  forest  so  remember this when you talk about him  that all of the people that you're  talking about in that time  uh hated him  and so the picture that he paints  is not the picture of a man who was with  him  be kind i i appreciate your comments  very much um  i i want to uh i want to emphasize that  i did not appro approach these  conclusions eagerly uh because i too  have descended or i have  my wife has ancestors who did fight with  uh pillow i mean with forest at uh fort  pillow uh familie